  "turns": [
    {
      "audioFilePath": "dialogue-turn-01.mp3",
      "endMs": 9446,
      "speaker": "novice",
      "startMs": 200,
      "text": "So quantum entanglement means two particles are linked like a magic walkie-talkie. I poke mine, yours reacts across the galaxy."
    },
    {
      "audioFilePath": "dialogue-turn-02.mp3",
      "endMs": 17867,
      "speaker": "teacher",
      "startMs": 9546,
      "text": "Quantum entanglement means their measurement outcomes are correlated. It does not mean you can send a message faster than light."
    },
    {
      "audioFilePath": "dialogue-turn-03.mp3",
      "endMs": 25133,
      "speaker": "novice",
      "startMs": 17967,
      "text": "But if I measure spin up here, the far particle is spin down there. That sounds instant."
    },
    {
      "audioFilePath": "dialogue-turn-04.mp3",
      "endMs": 33680,
      "speaker": "teacher",
      "startMs": 25233,
      "text": "Instant correlation, not controllable communication. You do not pick up or down. Measurement gives a random result."
    },
    {
      "audioFilePath": "dialogue-turn-05.mp3",
      "endMs": 39447,
      "speaker": "novice",
      "startMs": 33780,
      "text": "So I cannot encode a one by forcing my particle up?"
    },
    {
      "audioFilePath": "dialogue-turn-06.mp3",
      "endMs": 44957,
      "speaker": "teacher",
      "startMs": 39547,
      "text": "Exactly. Your local results look random no matter what the other lab does."
    },
    {
      "audioFilePath": "dialogue-turn-07.mp3",
      "endMs": 51703,
      "speaker": "novice",
      "startMs": 45057,
      "text": "Then what makes it quantum? Random plus random still sounds like a spreadsheet with anxiety."
    },
    {
      "audioFilePath": "dialogue-turn-08.mp3",
      "endMs": 59905,
      "speaker": "teacher",
      "startMs": 51803,
      "text": "The joint state cannot be split into two independent private states. The pair has a pattern neither particle owns alone."
    },
    {
      "audioFilePath": "dialogue-turn-09.mp3",
      "endMs": 64348,
      "speaker": "novice",
      "startMs": 60005,
      "text": "How do we know it is not just hidden instructions written earlier?"
    },
    {
      "audioFilePath": "dialogue-turn-10.mp3",
      "endMs": 72343,
      "speaker": "teacher",
      "startMs": 64448,
      "text": "Bell tests. Run many trials, compare the lists, and the correlations beat what classical hidden instruction cards can do."
    },
    {
      "audioFilePath": "dialogue-turn-11.mp3",
      "endMs": 76405,
      "speaker": "novice",
      "startMs": 72443,
      "text": "So the particles share a rule, but not a message?"
    },
    {
      "audioFilePath": "dialogue-turn-12.mp3",
      "endMs": 83041,
      "speaker": "teacher",
      "startMs": 76505,
      "text": "Right. Quantum entanglement is a shared statistical structure, not a cosmic direct message."
    },
    {
      "audioFilePath": "dialogue-turn-13.mp3",
      "endMs": 85675,
      "speaker": "novice",
      "startMs": 83141,
      "text": "But people say it breaks relativity."
    },
    {
      "audioFilePath": "dialogue-turn-14.mp3",
      "endMs": 93846,
      "speaker": "teacher",
      "startMs": 85775,
      "text": "It bothers intuition, not relativity. To learn the pattern, the labs still compare notes through ordinary light-speed communication."
    },
    {
      "audioFilePath": "dialogue-turn-15.mp3",
      "endMs": 99629,
      "speaker": "novice",
      "startMs": 93946,
      "text": "So quantum entanglement is real weirdness, but not remote control."
    },
    {
      "audioFilePath": "dialogue-turn-16.mp3",
      "endMs": 107846,
      "speaker": "teacher",
      "startMs": 99729,
      "text": "Finally. Spooky, yes. Telepathy, no. The universe kept the weird part and threw away the useful cheat code."
    }
  ],
